WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.540
Speaker 1: On what is quickly becoming an
historic day or a historic day, depending

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:10.590
on your grammatical preference, it's great
to be joined by Bill Share, who's a contributor

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:14.240
to the Washington Monthly Real Clear Politics
Politico.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:22.410
Bill, you know, let's not immediately include
the Ukraine Russia situation in our conversation

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:28.230
and sort of start with like, if this weren't
going on, what would your thoughts have been

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:36.440
on where the 2022 midterms seem to be looking
in terms of who is going to control the House

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:43.940
and Senate after November and then maybe will
integrate the question about Russia, Ukraine?

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:52.460
Speaker 3: Well, you know, in one respect,
you would say things don't look good for Democrats.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:01:01.150
Obviously, any time the president's party
is facing midterm, it tends to not go great.

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:08.579
We've had thirty six midterms since the Civil
War, and the president's party loses house

00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:10.729
seats in 33 of those 36.

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:15.650
Now, of course, a lot of times they lose control
of Congress because the margins are bigger,

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:17.189
but now the margin is five.

00:01:17.189 --> 00:01:22.880
So even the slightest loss you risk losing
control.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.329
And if Biden's at the same job approval in
November as he is today, which is about 42

00:01:28.329 --> 00:01:32.649
percent, that's not going to be a good midterm.

00:01:32.649 --> 00:01:36.770
Having said that, there are those three exceptions.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:40.650
And there's also a fourth exception where
the Democrats, where Democrats lost less than

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.450
five seats, that was 1962.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:48.799
So those four exceptions are 1934, 1962, 1998,
2002.

00:01:48.799 --> 00:01:52.880
So what's what's the thread in those exceptions?

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:59.299
Thirty four is FDR his first term he's tackling
the Great Depression 62 Cuban Missile Crisis

00:01:59.299 --> 00:02:04.130
is solved ten days before the midterm 1998.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:08.039
Republicans are moving towards impeaching
Bill Clinton, and there's a lot of talk in

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:11.260
the era of constitutional crisis and 2002.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:17.530
We're still in the shadow of of nine 911 crisis
is is the thread of those four exceptions.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:23.060
If you're in the president's party and there's
a crisis and you're seen as handling that

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:27.310
crisis well, right, you can beat those odds
right now.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:34.660
As it stands today, we have not counting Ukraine
two big crises that are intertwined the pandemic

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:36.420
and inflation.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:41.920
Today, Biden is not seen as handling those
well, so that's not becoming a plus.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.730
It is certainly possible that by November,
the pandemic is going to look a lot better

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:48.739
and inflation is going to look a lot better.

00:02:48.739 --> 00:02:53.260
We can't say for certain today it has definitely
already winding down, and we just don't know

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.131
if one of the new variants are going to kick
up between now and then.

00:02:56.131 --> 00:02:58.980
It's going to throw us for another loop.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.959
But and Ukraine is a third crisis.

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:02.480
And again, I'm not.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.690
It's hardly one.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.760
Can I glibly say, Oh, Biden is going to take
care of this fantastically?

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.730
It's a very, very complicated problem.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:11.590
And even the most fantastic president.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.930
Might I handle it in the most perfect of ways?

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:19.680
Speaker 1: But to add to that bill, I mean,
no matter what Biden does, Republicans will

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:20.680
blame him.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:21.829
I mean, they're already doing it.

00:03:21.829 --> 00:03:26.730
Last night, Trump with Laura Ingraham saying
the problem here is Biden and the rigged election

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:31.030
and people coming over to the US-Mexico border
that he said that's the cause of what's happening

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:32.280
in Russia, Ukraine.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:37.299
You have a bunch of people already saying
he it's his fault and to some degree, that

00:03:37.299 --> 00:03:39.660
